<!--

  ViewModel Lab — a fixture for host-side ViewModel instance editing.

  A host (unit tests, a demo app, the YUP bridge) can read and write *every*
  view model property type here and watch the picture follow: each value below
  is bound into the UI, so a write that took effect is a pixel that changed and
  `--data` / `--data-dump` can assert on it.

  Layout: a header bar (title, subtitle, the two colour swatches, live `count`),
  then two columns of cards, then a row pairing the nested artboard with the
  list.

    header    `title`, `count`, `accent` and (nested) `details.tint` swatches
    col A     strings & numbers — `note` + `count` values, and the three
              write-back pills (`count +1`, `write note`, `status: failed`)
              nested view model — `details` through depth-2 bind paths
              asset image — `icon` bound to Image.assetId
              trigger — `ping` bound to the Flash state
    col B     progress — number -> bound width (DataConverterRangeMapper)
              status — custom enum -> EnumToUint chain + a state per value
              enabled — boolean -> two authored transitions + a write-back click
              list length — DataConverterListToLength -> ToString chain
    bottom    artboard `panel` (plus `panelData`, its own instance) and the list:
              `rows` — three Row instances, each with its own label, amount,
              done flag and `itemIndex`

  Writes that travel the other way — scene -> host — are the four listeners in
  the state machine, plus the row artboard's `done` flags. Click them and read
  the instance back:

    - `count +1` pill      `count`   += 1      scripted listener action
    - `write note` pill    `note`    = literal ListenerViewModelChange
    - `status: failed`     `status`  = failed  ListenerViewModelChange
    - the switch           `enabled` = not it  scripted listener action

  The two scripted ones are scripts for a reason worth knowing: a converter on
  the *read* context of a `ListenerViewModelChange` does not survive — the read
  resolves to nothing and the write lands as the bindable's default — so
  "negate the current value" and "add one to the current value" cannot be built
  from `DataConverterBooleanNegate`/`DataConverterOperationValue`. A plain
  two-context bindable with no converter does pass the source's value through,
  and a literal write (`propertyValue=` on the bindable, no read context) is
  reliable; see count_plus_one.luau and toggle_enabled.luau.

  Four things worth knowing when testing:
    - every artboard needs a `defaultStateMachineId`, or no bind is ever
      pushed and nothing here reacts;
    - `changed` in a `--data-dump` is cumulative since bind, and only tracks
      writes *into* a property — an inert bind reads identical to a live one;
    - `--data=` paths are relative to the bound instance and every segment is a
      property *name*: `progress=1`, `details/weight=11.5`, `status=failed`;
    - a `Text` (or `Shape`, or `Image`) only joins its parent's flex flow if
      it carries a `LayoutParticipant`. Without one it keeps its own x/y --
      measured from the parent's top-left -- and is stretched to the parent's
      box, so every sibling stacks in the same corner and the padding is
      ignored. Every `Text` here has one, `fixed` to the width and height on
      the `Text` itself; keep the two in step when editing;
    - in this format a child wider than its container's content box is not
      clipped, it is *squeezed*: a `Text` whose authored width overflows (the
      card's padding and the row gaps count) collapses to a one-character slot
      and then wraps one letter per line. Every width below was worked out
      against its container and there is slack everywhere — the comments spell
      the arithmetic out. Keep that true when editing, or the text goes
      vertical again.

-->
